86436014 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436024 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436016 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436024 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436020 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436024 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436024 1077 "selection" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436024 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436008 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436024 1079 #uuid "30fcae88-a1e2-5970-b696-299270bb46fe" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436011 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436017 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436019 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436021 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436022 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1077 "selection" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436011 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436025 1079 #uuid "adf8aa6a-75ee-5eec-b556-6b1e3d610f4a" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436024 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436026 1069 "q-128450" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436026 1071 "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(-2,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}?$" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436026 1072 #uuid "563f43ed-8e03-5890-8ba0-fe3a95b9c80d" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436026 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,-1) & =4{(-2)}^{2}+9{(-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4(4)+9(1) \\\\  & =16+9 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the $25$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=25.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436023 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436027 1069 "q-128448" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436027 1071 "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(3,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{x-y}?$" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436027 1072 #uuid "af624627-f936-5c7e-a5ee-772c5d7da48f" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436027 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =\\frac{2}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4 & =x-y \\\\ y & =x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the line $y=x-4.$" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436025 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436028 1069 "q-129114" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436028 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={y}^{2}?$" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436028 1072 #uuid "c5ccf563-8a57-5f1f-ad06-ef72dc726ab7" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true] [17592186436028 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [0,\\infty ),$ the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png)" 13194139534787 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-000302c2-3c84-5577-bb85-c11cc3bf9cee" 17592186436008 "answer-0329b153-bf57-5eea-8e4f-f3f01337857b" 17592186436009 "answer-0bb60a10-e100-54a3-a098-70fb2ea763e7" 17592186436010 "answer-19118c24-d5da-5954-9e93-75cfa8bacbe5" 17592186436011 "answer-44caf6b5-57f9-522a-a950-aba744b9e3c3" 17592186436012 "answer-6d6f7295-9d6c-517b-9e6e-6ee1b2c02c7e" 17592186436013 "answer-6df11da8-be9b-5344-b62d-79a4559efa3c" 17592186436014 "answer-9a0b0721-1fd0-5994-a36a-82e40db9769c" 17592186436015 "answer-a513aedd-cbc7-5326-bb5e-498f3f229294" 17592186436016 "answer-b68c227f-79b4-539e-9f21-17aa72aae1be" 17592186436017 "answer-cc8a8126-4229-53cd-bece-781ab226e64b" 17592186436018 "answer-d4fdc308-acd9-599d-98f4-61b51923dcf4" 17592186436019 "answer-df1cdfb6-d415-5196-8163-5b0778c79a74" 17592186436020 "answer-ea4ea415-1a42-5ab9-a4f6-74d5d9c47e2c" 17592186436021 "answer-f049c9a7-cc49-5c3f-a4df-0714b2f9933a" 17592186436022 "answer-field-11ea0465-98a8-532d-bb2d-293a976cf74c" 17592186436023 "answer-field-30fcae88-a1e2-5970-b696-299270bb46fe" 17592186436024 "answer-field-adf8aa6a-75ee-5eec-b556-6b1e3d610f4a" 17592186436025 "question-563f43ed-8e03-5890-8ba0-fe3a95b9c80d" 1759218